WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.693
So the irony in these systems is that...

00:00:04.693 --> 00:00:06.583
the irony in predictive systems

00:00:06.583 --> 00:00:09.103
is that they're actually unpredictable at least

00:00:09.103 --> 00:00:11.653
at first when machine learning systems

00:00:11.653 --> 00:00:14.442
are new they're often inaccurate

00:00:14.442 --> 00:00:15.883
which is actually not what we expect from our

00:00:15.883 --> 00:00:18.013
things. You know, 60% or 70%

00:00:18.013 --> 00:00:20.202
accuracy is actually really good for a

00:00:20.202 --> 00:00:21.733
first pass at a machine learning system

00:00:21.733 --> 00:00:23.713
but even ninety percent accuracy isn't

00:00:23.713 --> 00:00:25.753
enough to make a predictive

00:00:25.753 --> 00:00:28.062
system feel right. Because it's making

00:00:28.062 --> 00:00:30.132
decisions all the time. And it's going to be

00:00:30.132 --> 00:00:33.013
making mistakes all the time. It's fine

00:00:33.013 --> 00:00:34.242
if your house is a couple of degrees

00:00:34.242 --> 00:00:35.953
cooler than you'd like or warmer

00:00:35.953 --> 00:00:38.952
whatever. But if your wheelchair refuses

00:00:38.952 --> 00:00:40.843
to go to a drinking fountain that's next to

00:00:40.843 --> 00:00:42.193
a door because it's been trained

00:00:42.193 --> 00:00:44.833
on doors, and it can't tell that what you're

00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:45.823
saying is "I want to go to that thing

00:00:45.823 --> 00:00:47.923
next to the door," not "to the door,"

00:00:47.923 --> 00:00:50.533
that's a real problem. And for

00:00:50.533 --> 00:00:52.333
all the times the system gets things

00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:54.972
right, you judge it on its mistakes.

00:00:54.972 --> 00:00:59.532
And mistakes create anxiety. And anxiety

00:00:59.532 --> 00:01:01.183
is an incredibly heavy kind of cognitive

00:01:01.183 --> 00:01:03.793
load to bear. And a little doubt

00:01:03.793 --> 00:01:06.793
about whether a supposedly smart system

00:01:06.793 --> 00:01:08.683
is going to do the right thing is enough to

00:01:08.683 --> 00:01:10.693
turn a user experience that's right most

00:01:10.693 --> 00:01:12.373
of the time, into one that's more

00:01:12.373 --> 00:01:15.642
trouble than it's worth. The last

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:17.293
issue comes as a result of these previous two:

00:01:17.293 --> 00:01:19.903
Control. How do we maintain some level

00:01:19.903 --> 00:01:21.913
of control over these devices when

00:01:21.913 --> 00:01:24.253
their behavior is by definition statistical

00:01:24.253 --> 00:01:27.492
and unpredictable? On the one hand, you know,

00:01:27.492 --> 00:01:29.532
you can mangle your device's predictive

00:01:29.532 --> 00:01:32.142
behavior by giving it too much data.

00:01:32.142 --> 00:01:34.242
I visited Nest one time

00:01:34.242 --> 00:01:36.043
in their early days. And they told me that

00:01:36.043 --> 00:01:37.093
none of the Nests in their office

00:01:37.093 --> 00:01:38.803
actually worked. Because they were constantly

00:01:38.803 --> 00:01:40.453
fiddling with them.

00:01:40.453 --> 00:01:41.922
They're constantly, like, playing around with...

00:01:41.922 --> 00:01:43.572
And in machine learning this is

00:01:43.572 --> 00:01:47.623
what's called "over-training". On the other hand,

00:01:47.623 --> 00:01:49.903
if I have no direct way to control it other

00:01:49.903 --> 00:01:51.523
than through my own behavior, how do i

00:01:51.523 --> 00:01:54.132
adjust it? So Amazon and Netflix's

00:01:54.132 --> 00:01:55.782
recommendation systems which are

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:57.672
machine learning systems for predicting what

00:01:57.672 --> 00:01:59.083
you might like... they've been around for

00:01:59.083 --> 00:02:01.693
a long time. They give you some context

00:02:01.693 --> 00:02:04.152
about why they recommended

00:02:04.152 --> 00:02:05.383
something, why they're doing something.

00:02:05.383 --> 00:02:07.543
But what if my only interface

00:02:07.543 --> 00:02:11.683
is a garden hose? What do I do? How do I

00:02:11.683 --> 00:02:16.723
get it to stop spraying water? As interesting

00:02:16.723 --> 00:02:18.193
as these issues are, I actually think

00:02:18.193 --> 00:02:19.963
that most importantly what they represent

00:02:19.963 --> 00:02:21.313
is that we're entering into a

00:02:21.313 --> 00:02:23.653
new relationship with our device ecosystem.

00:02:23.653 --> 00:02:27.853
We're experiencing a sea change in

00:02:27.853 --> 00:02:30.613
our relationship to the built world. So think

00:02:30.613 --> 00:02:32.623
of a sewing machine. It's really complex.

00:02:32.623 --> 00:02:34.843
But it basically acts in response to us.

00:02:34.843 --> 00:02:40.183
Computers acting autonomously erode this

00:02:40.183 --> 00:02:43.783
simple tool to user relationship. At the

00:02:43.783 --> 00:02:45.583
dawn of computing in the late 1940s,

00:02:45.583 --> 00:02:47.833
cyberneticists like Norbert Wiener

00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:50.413
philosophized about this

00:02:50.413 --> 00:02:52.093
increasingly complex relationship

00:02:52.093 --> 00:02:54.493
between people and computers. And how it

00:02:54.493 --> 00:02:56.323
was fundamentally different than

00:02:56.323 --> 00:02:57.853
the way that we've interacted with other kinds

00:02:57.853 --> 00:03:00.133
of machines other kinds of tools.

00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:02.023
So developers who are working in

00:03:02.023 --> 00:03:04.063
supervisory control of

00:03:04.063 --> 00:03:06.103
manufacturing machine--so basically,

00:03:06.103 --> 00:03:08.743
you know, things that build cars, things that...

00:03:08.743 --> 00:03:10.783
are giant robots in factories...

00:03:10.783 --> 00:03:12.853
they've actually been having

00:03:12.853 --> 00:03:14.623
to deal with these questions

00:03:14.623 --> 00:03:18.133
very pragmatically for a very long time.

00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:20.053
Now thanks to the Internet of Things, it's

00:03:20.053 --> 00:03:24.493
everybody's problem. So this is a

00:03:24.493 --> 00:03:27.313
diagram by the great Tom Sheridan who was

00:03:27.313 --> 00:03:29.173
a brilliant engineer and

00:03:29.173 --> 00:03:32.233
Bill Verplank. This is from 1978. In which

00:03:32.233 --> 00:03:33.313
what they're trying to do is

00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:34.783
they're trying to illustrate four ways

00:03:34.783 --> 00:03:36.973
that semi-autonomous computers and

00:03:36.973 --> 00:03:38.983
humans can work together to solve a

00:03:38.983 --> 00:03:41.383
problem. This actually comes out of

00:03:41.383 --> 00:03:42.733
an amazing project when they were--

00:03:42.733 --> 00:03:45.673
in the freaking 70s!--they were doing these like

00:03:45.673 --> 00:03:49.033
semi-autonomous submersible robots that

00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:50.593
would like go deep underwater and...

00:03:50.593 --> 00:03:54.703
they were so deep underwater that... you'd

00:03:54.703 --> 00:03:56.683
have to control them from the surface. But

00:03:56.683 --> 00:03:58.303
you didn't have like really great control.

00:03:58.303 --> 00:03:59.593
You have to tell them what to do... and...

00:03:59.593 --> 00:04:02.113
this is like 01975 they were doing this.

00:04:02.113 --> 00:04:04.993
Sheridan continued to think about this.

00:04:04.993 --> 00:04:08.773
He was a smart guy. By 02000 he'd actually

00:04:08.773 --> 00:04:11.203
expanded that idea to create this

00:04:11.203 --> 00:04:13.393
framework. And he defined a spectrum of

00:04:13.393 --> 00:04:15.582
responsibility between people and

00:04:15.582 --> 00:04:18.613
computers. So it ranges from the top,

00:04:18.613 --> 00:04:21.523
#1... so i'll read it for the folks

00:04:21.523 --> 00:04:21.763
in back

00:04:21.763 --> 00:04:24.313
#1 "The computer offers no assistance,

00:04:24.313 --> 00:04:27.253
humans must make all decisions and actions."

00:04:27.253 --> 00:04:30.403
So it ranges from #1, where humans do

00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:31.603
all the work--that's like, you know,

00:04:31.603 --> 00:04:34.003
you writing an essay (unless you're in school

00:04:34.003 --> 00:04:37.603
in which case you Google for it) or

00:04:37.603 --> 00:04:41.653
you knitting. To #10, computers doing all the work:

00:04:41.653 --> 00:04:43.483
"The computer decides everything and

00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:46.813
acts autonomously, ignoring the human."

00:04:46.813 --> 00:04:50.113
That's your car's fuel injection controller.

00:04:50.113 --> 00:04:55.663
So, of course, the goal is to get

00:04:55.663 --> 00:04:58.333
to something down here near 9 or 10.

00:04:58.333 --> 00:05:00.823
That's kind of the maximum reduction

00:05:00.823 --> 00:05:03.553
on cognitive load. But for a system to

00:05:03.553 --> 00:05:05.803
qualify for that it needs to be

00:05:05.803 --> 00:05:08.053
very stable. Its effects need to

00:05:08.053 --> 00:05:10.783
be very predictable. And more importantly

00:05:10.783 --> 00:05:13.843
its role needs to be very well embedded

00:05:13.843 --> 00:05:17.563
and understood in society. We trust it

00:05:17.563 --> 00:05:21.013
to do that thing. At the airport we trust

00:05:21.013 --> 00:05:23.053
the monorail computer to work

00:05:23.053 --> 00:05:25.303
without human intervention. But

00:05:25.303 --> 00:05:27.523
we don't trust the plane auto pilot to do that.

00:05:27.523 --> 00:05:29.893
Even though, as I understand that,

00:05:29.893 --> 00:05:31.363
planes can basically fly themselves these days.

00:05:31.363 --> 00:05:34.843
it's not socially embedded yet.

00:05:34.843 --> 00:05:39.343
Predictive IOT devices generally fall

00:05:39.343 --> 00:05:41.503
between 5 to 7 today.

00:05:41.503 --> 00:05:43.663
And probably for the near future. The problem

00:05:43.663 --> 00:05:45.703
is that this is the exact range

00:05:45.703 --> 00:05:47.533
where you're maximizing someone's cognitive

00:05:47.533 --> 00:05:49.243
load. Because you're making them make

00:05:49.243 --> 00:05:51.433
a lot of decisions, but not necessarily

00:05:51.433 --> 00:05:53.743
doing all the work for them. So

00:05:53.743 --> 00:05:55.843
the result of the automation had really

00:05:55.843 --> 00:05:57.703
better be worth it, in order for

00:05:57.703 --> 00:06:00.733
that system to work. Moreover

00:06:00.733 --> 00:06:02.593
that relationship fundamentally undermines

00:06:02.593 --> 00:06:05.053
what we expect from our tools. Because

00:06:05.053 --> 00:06:06.553
when that tool is trying to anticipate

00:06:06.553 --> 00:06:10.063
what we're trying to do and trying to get

00:06:10.063 --> 00:06:12.343
in front of us in doing it,

00:06:12.343 --> 00:06:14.473
it fundamentally changes our working

00:06:14.473 --> 00:06:25.773
relationship with it.

00:06:25.773 --> 00:06:27.832
you

